---

<LayoutDefault
  title="Smart Lawn — My Garden prototype — Gordan Grgić"
  description="From a 50 m² lawn to My Garden: a working soil-monitoring prototype with root-zone visuals, moisture history and a two-probe ESP32 / RS485 hardware plan. Try the simulated demo."
>
  <div class="lawn-notes">
    <header class="lawn-heading">
      <a href="/projects/">← All project notes</a>
      <p class="folio-label">HARDWARE + SOFTWARE + GARDEN / FIELD NOTES 03</p>
      <h1>Smart Lawn</h1>
      <p class="project-state">
        In development <span>Working software prototype</span>
      </p>
      <p class="lead">A small lawn. A closer look beneath the surface.</p>
      <p>
        What started as an idea for watering my 49 m² lawn is becoming <strong>grgaGrow</strong> — a mobile soil-monitoring
        app, with two wired probes and an ESP32 controller planned for the garden. The software can already be explored; the
        hardware connection is the next step.
      </p>
      <div class="project-actions">
        <a
          class="demo-link live-link"
          href="/demos/my-garden/?public=grgagrow"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">View Live garden ↗</a
        >
        <a class="demo-link" href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Try the interactive demo ↗</a
        >
        <a href="#lawn-hardware">Explore the hardware ↓</a>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Live: public readings, regional weather and sun/shade · Demo: simulated readings · No account needed
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="dashboard-figure">
      <a
        href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=demo"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open the My Garden interactive demo in a new tab"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/my-garden-dashboard.jpg"
          alt="My Garden prototype showing Sunny corner and Garden edge soil cards, root illustrations, moisture and temperature, with a visible demo-mode label"
          width="1265"
          height="949"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        THE WORKING INTERFACE / My Garden, October 2026. All readings are simulated; roots are illustrative.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-software">
      <p class="folio-label">01 / SOFTWARE YOU CAN EXPLORE</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-software">See the soil. Follow the changes.</h2>
      <p>
        I wanted the dashboard to feel understandable at a glance, while keeping the detail close by. A botanical view
        puts the root zone beside each reading; a compact view brings the numbers forward. Moisture, temperature and
        measurement age stay together.
      </p>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">TWO WAYS TO READ IT</span>
          <h3>Visual or compact.</h3>
          <p>
            Switch between illustrated sensor cards and a denser view. A separate moisture scale shows Dry, Getting dry,
            Target and Wet.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">A RECORD OVER TIME</span>
          <h3>From a moment to a trend.</h3>
          <p>
            Explore 24-hour and 7-day history, select either sensor and export the readings as CSV. Missing data stays
            visible as gaps.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">FOUR DEMO SCENARIOS</span>
          <h3>Try a different day.</h3>
          <p>
            Balanced, Drying, Watering and Data loss let you explore how the interface responds before any probe is
            connected.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">SETTINGS PER SENSOR</span>
          <h3>Make the ranges explicit.</h3>
          <p>
            Adjust each sensor’s thresholds and see how they affect the display. Defaults are examples; real soil needs
            observation and calibration.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="stack">React · TypeScript · Vite · Responsive interface</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-reliability">
      <p class="folio-label">02 / THE ENGINEERING BEHIND THE SCREEN</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-reliability">What if a reading never arrives?</h2>
      <p>
        A reassuring number is only useful if it can be trusted. The prototype checks freshness and time quality,
        preserves gaps in the history, and withholds watering suggestions when readings are unreliable. A dry trend must
        remain valid for an uninterrupted 30-minute window before the current logic suggests watering.
      </p>
      <div class="engineering-note">
        <p class="small-label">DEVELOPMENT EXAMPLE / A LOST ACKNOWLEDGEMENT</p>
        <h3>Send again. Keep one reading.</h3>
        <p>
          A measurement can reach the database even when its acknowledgement never reaches the device. The local queue
          model and ingestion code are tested together: resend that measurement, keep a single database record, then
          remove only the readings that have been acknowledged.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          This verifies the software model locally. ESP32 firmware and real flash power-loss testing still come next.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        grgaGrow offers two public views. Demo follows the published Live setup with simulated sensor readings and no
        editable settings. Live is a read-only window into selected garden measurements, seven days of history, regional
        weather and an approximate sun/shade model. The owner uses a separate signed-in workspace for settings, alerts
        and the garden diary. Device ingestion and physical sensor operation still require hardware verification; Live
        shows waiting for data until real readings arrive.
      </p>
      <p class="stack">Supabase / PostgreSQL · Access rules · Idempotent ingestion · Local automated checks</p>
      <p class="note">
        The public feed excludes exact coordinates and private settings. The owner chooses what to publish and can
        disable Live. Watering advice uses preliminary user-defined thresholds, not validated agronomic guidance;
        automatic irrigation is not active.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-hardware">
      <p class="folio-label">03 / HARDWARE DIRECTION</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-hardware">Two probes. One path to the dashboard.</h2>
      <p>
        The current hardware selection uses two wired probes and a controller with an RS485 interface. The controller
        and probes have been ordered. They have not yet been connected, calibrated or tested in the lawn.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        Reference photos below illustrate the component types. Models and appearance may differ from the ordered parts.
      </p>
      <div class="hardware-gallery" aria-label="Reference photos of hardware component types">
        {
          hardwareGallery.map((item, index) => (
            <figure class="hardware-photo">
              <img
                src={`/images/projects/smart-lawn/hardware/${item.image}`}
                width={item.width}
                height={item.height}
                alt={item.alt}
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
                data-index={index}
                data-group="smart-lawn-hardware"
                data-title={`${item.title} — ${item.detail}. Reference image of this component type.`}
                tabindex="0"
                role="button"
                aria-label={`Enlarge ${item.title}`}
              />
              <figcaption>
                <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                <p>{item.detail}</p>
                {item.source && (
                  <a href={item.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    Photo: {item.vendor} ↗
                  </a>
                )}
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Click a photo to enlarge. Each image links to its source. Assembly and specification checks are still ahead.
      </p>
      <div
        class="architecture"
        aria-label="Planned data path: two soil probes connect via RS485 and Modbus RTU to an ESP32 controller, then via Wi-Fi and HTTPS to ingestion, PostgreSQL and the authenticated My Garden app."
      >
        <div class="probe-row">
          <span>Probe 01<br /><small>Moisture + temperature</small></span><span
            >Probe 02<br /><small>Moisture + temperature + pH*</small></span
          >
        </div>
        <p class="flow-label">↓ RS485 / Modbus RTU</p>
        <div class="architecture-node">ESP32-S3 controller</div>
        <p class="flow-label">↓ Wi-Fi / HTTPS</p>
        <div class="architecture-node">Ingestion → PostgreSQL</div>
        <p class="flow-label">↓ Authenticated access</p>
        <div class="architecture-node app-node">My Garden</div>
        <p class="note architecture-note">Planned connection · Backend code prepared locally · *pH is experimental</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hardware-grid">
        {
          hardware.map((item) => (
            <article class="hardware-item">
              <div class="hardware-label">
                <>
                  <span>{item.number} / HARDWARE</span>
                  <span>{item.status}</span>
                </>
              </div>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.description}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-site">
      <p class="folio-label">04 / THE REAL GARDEN</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-site">A practical question, close to home.</h2>
      <p>
        I still water the lawn manually. Sun, shade and soil conditions can make nearby areas behave differently. I want
        to compare how quickly they dry, how the soil temperature changes, and how long water takes to reach a probe.
      </p>
      <figure class="lawn-cover">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/lawn.jpg"
          alt="My 50 square metre lawn with a robotic mower, the future test site for the two soil probes"
          width="1600"
          height="1200"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          data-group="cover"
        />
        <figcaption>
          THE TEST SITE / My 50 m² lawn. Monitoring is not installed yet; placement and cable routes must also suit the
          robotic mower.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        This is the part I enjoy: connecting the interface to something physical, making the measurements
        understandable, and checking the whole path from a sensor in the ground to a decision on the screen.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-next">
      <p class="folio-label">05 / CURRENT STATE & NEXT STEPS</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-next">Measure first. Automate later.</h2>
      <ol class="roadmap">
        <li>
          <strong>Working locally</strong><p>
            Mobile interface, simulated scenarios, history, settings and CSV export. Backend and queue behaviour have
            local automated checks.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hardware integration next</strong><p>
            Verify the delivered board and probe documentation, implement the firmware, then test communication,
            calibration and recovery on the bench.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connect and observe</strong><p>
            Validate the hosted backend, collect real readings and compare both locations through ordinary watering and
            weather changes.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consider irrigation</strong><p>
            Only after reliable monitoring: test a valve, shut-off behaviour and watering limits. Weather and
            notifications are available in the app; automatic irrigation remains future work.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="closing-demo">
        <div>
          <h3>Take a look beneath the surface.</h3><p>View the public garden, or explore the simulated demo.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="project-actions">
          <a class="demo-link" href="/demos/my-garden/?public=grgagrow" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >View Live ↗</a
          ><a class="demo-link" href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Try demo ↗</a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Project snapshot: 30 October 2026 · Live measurements pending hardware connection · Demo uses simulated data
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</LayoutDefault>

<style>
  .lawn-notes {
    font-family: var(--body-font, 'IBM Plex Sans', sans-serif);
  }
  .lawn-heading > a {
    font: 12px var(--note-font);
    color: inherit;
  }
  .folio-label,
  .small-label {
    font: 10px/1.8 var(--note-font);
    letter-spacing: 1.3px;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }
  .folio-label {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  h1 {
    font:
      48px/1.2 'MyFont',
      monospace;
    margin: 24px 0;
  }
  h2 {
    font: 24px/1.4 var(--note-font);
    margin: 16px 0;
  }
  h3 {
    font: 16px/1.5 var(--note-font);
    margin: 10px 0;
  }
  p,
  li {
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 23px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  .project-state {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
    align-items: center;
    font: 12px/1.6 var(--note-font);
    color: #39776c;
  }
  .project-state span {
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
    padding: 5px 8px;
  }
  .project-actions {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 18px 26px;
    margin: 26px 0 12px;
  }
  .project-actions > a {
    font: 12px/1.8 var(--note-font);
  }
  a {
    color: inherit;
  }
  a.demo-link {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 12px 18px;
    background: #2e6055;
    color: #fff;
    font: 12px/1.8 var(--note-font);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  a.demo-link:hover {
    background: #234a42;
    color: #fff;
  }
  a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid #39776c;
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }
  figure {
    margin: 28px 0;
  }
  figure img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
  }
